--TEST--
A `static` property whose type admits `null` needs no initializer and starts the request at `null`; a subclass redeclaring one gets a second storage — ADR 0022 § 2, and `docs/adr/README.md` § *Decisions taken at project start* for the lifetime
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Cache {
    // No initializer, and none required: ADR 0022 § 2 exempts a nullable
    // declaration, so the slot is armed with `null` when the request starts.
    // This is a deliberate Novis answer rather than PHP's — PHP leaves a typed
    // static *uninitialized* and throws on the read, which is exactly the
    // "reading a binding that may not have reached its initialiser" state
    // `rule:types/declaration` does not have.
    public static ?string $entry;
    public static ?int $hits;

    public static function seed(): void {
        self::$entry = "warm";
        self::$hits = 1;
    }
}

// Redeclaring in a subclass is a second storage, exactly as it is in PHP:
// the checker resolves each access to the class that *declares* the name it
// finds, so these two never alias.
class Layered extends Cache {
    public static ?string $entry;
}

echo Cache::$entry ?? "cold", "\n";
echo (Cache::$hits ?? 0) as string, "\n";

Cache::seed();
echo Cache::$entry ?? "cold", "\n";
echo (Cache::$hits ?? 0) as string, "\n";

// `Layered::$entry` names its own declaration, so seeding the parent's left
// it alone; `Cache::$entry` still holds what `seed` put there.
echo Layered::$entry ?? "cold", "\n";
Layered::$entry = "own";
echo Layered::$entry ?? "cold", " ", Cache::$entry ?? "cold", "\n";
--EXPECT--
cold
0
warm
1
cold
own warm
